NAME
    grep - filter lines matching a regular expression

SYNOPSIS
    grep [-i] [-v] [-n] [-c] [-q] <pattern> [text...]

DESCRIPTION
    Filters lines matching <pattern> using Rust's regex engine. Standard
    regular expression syntax is supported, with non-quantifier '{' and
    '}' treated as literals; look-around assertions and back-references
    are not supported.

    Reads piped standard input when present; otherwise searches trailing
    literal [text...] joined by spaces. Does not read file paths
    directly (pipe cat(1) into grep instead).

OPTIONS
    -i  Perform case-insensitive matching.
    -v  Invert match: select lines that do not match <pattern>.
    -n  Prefix each matching line with its 1-based line number ('N:').
    -c  Print only the count of matching lines.
    -q  Quiet mode: produce no output and report match via exit status.

EXAMPLES
    watch log 1 | grep -v poll
    if regs | grep -q PC; then echo has-pc; fi
    cat /Logs/run.log | grep -in error

EXIT STATUS
    0   One or more lines matched (or usage printed with no pattern).
    1   No lines matched, or invalid regular expression.

SEE ALSO
    sed(1), awk(1), find(1), wc(1)
